.nh
.TH "TESTX" "1" "Jan 2024" "gnux" "gnux manual"

.SH NAME
testx - Check file types and compare values.


.SH SYNOPSIS
\fBtestx EXPRESSION\fP


.SH DESCRIPTION
testx evaluates EXPRESSION and exits with status 0 when it is true and 1 when it is false; it prints nothing. With no EXPRESSION it is false. Strings: -n STRING (not empty), -z STRING (empty), STRING alone (not empty), S1 = S2 (also ==), S1 != S2, and S1 < S2 or S1 > S2, comparing bytes (the C locale). Integers: N1 -eq, -ne, -lt, -le, -gt or -ge N2, where an integer is an optional sign and decimal digits of any length, with surrounding blanks allowed. Files: -e (exists), -f (regular file), -d (directory), -h or -L (symbolic link, not followed), -b and -c (block and character devices), -p (named pipe), -S (socket), -s (size above zero), -r, -w and -x (readable, writable, executable by you), -u, -g and -k (set-user-ID, set-group-ID and sticky bits), -O and -G (owned by your effective user or group), -t FD (descriptor FD is a terminal), and F1 -nt, -ot or -ef F2 (newer, older, or the same file). Expressions combine with ! (not), -a (and, binding tighter), -o (or) and parentheses, which must be separate arguments (quote them from the shell). As POSIX requires, one to four arguments are read by their count first, so testx -n = -n compares two strings. A malformed expression is diagnosed with GNU's wording ('a': unary operator expected, invalid integer 'x', missing argument after '-f', extra argument 'c') and exits with status 2. As in GNU test, -l STRING may stand for either integer of a comparison and means the length of STRING (testx -l "$name" -gt 8). The same tool answers to [ EXPRESSION ], with the closing ] required: as gnux [ ... ], as a link named [, and inside gnux shell; without the ] it reports missing ']' and exits 2. Differences from GNU test: a lone --help or --version prints help or the version where GNU test would treat it as a non-empty string, and off Unix -O and -G are always false while -r, -w and -x are read from the permission bits.

.PP
EXIT STATUS.
  0  EXPRESSION is true
  1  EXPRESSION is false, or missing
  2  EXPRESSION is malformed (diagnosed on standard error)

.PP
HELP. testx --help shows the options and the first examples; gnux help testx prints the full manual, and gnux help testx OPTION explains one option.

.PP
SEE ALSO. exprx, truex, falsex, gnux shell.

.PP
PERMISSIONS. Reads file metadata only: -e, -f, -d and the other file tests need search permission on the directories leading to the file; -r, -w and -x ask the operating system whether you could read, write or execute it, without trying. Nothing is ever modified.

.PP
USE CASES.
  - Checking that a file or directory exists before using it in a script.
  - Branching on whether a variable is empty with -z or -n.
  - Comparing version numbers or counts with -lt, -ge and the other integer tests.
  - Rebuilding only when a source file is newer than its output with -nt.
  - Checking that a script is writing to a terminal with -t 1 before using colors.
  - Guarding a destructive step with && in the portable gnux shell.
  - Verifying permissions (-r, -w, -x) before a deployment step runs.


.SH OPTIONS
\fB--help\fP[=false]
	display this help and exit

.PP
\fB--version\fP[=false]
	output version information and exit


.SH EXAMPLE
.EX
  # Check that a file exists
  testx -e config.json && echox found

  # Check for a directory
  testx -d build || mkdirx build

  # Check that a variable is not empty
  testx -n "$NAME" && echox "hello, $NAME"

  # Compare two strings
  testx "$BRANCH" = main && echox "on main"

  # Compare two numbers
  testx 10 -gt 9 && echox bigger

  # Rebuild only when the source is newer than the output
  testx main.go -nt app && go build -o app .

  # Combine conditions with -a and -o
  testx -f go.mod -a -d .git && echox 'a Go repository'

  # Negate and group (parentheses are separate, quoted arguments)
  testx ! '(' -e a.lock -o -e b.lock ')' && echox unlocked

  # Check that a file is not empty
  testx -s report.csv || echox 'report is empty'

  # Check whether standard output is a terminal
  testx -t 1 && echox interactive

  # See the status of a malformed expression (2)
  testx 1 -eq one; echox "exit status $?"

  # Guard a step in the portable gnux shell
  gnux shell -c 'testx -d dist && rmx -r dist'
.EE
